{
  "_comment": "Shared cross-language vectors for IfcGridPlacement across IFC4X3 (#9026 item 14). IFC4X3 puts the inherited PlacementRelTo first. Below IFC4X3 the placement sits in the frame of the IfcGrid owning its axes, and in IFC4X3 $ means the world frame, so an upgrade names the owning grid's ObjectPlacement. Run by packages/export/src/schema-grid-frames.parity.test.ts and rust/export/tests/schema_grid_frames_parity.rs. The IFC2X3 cases after them pin #9209: IFC2X3 places exactly one product per placement (PlacesObject SET [1:1]), so a grid placement's only local child folds into it where IFC2X3 can say the same thing, else comes free as a plain placement at the same point, and a shared placement is copied for each further product. Both harnesses run that IFC2X3 pass on every IFC2X3 output.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "why": "the owning grid's placement becomes PlacementRelTo",
      "from": "IFC4",
      "to": "IFC4X3",
      "records": [
        "#20=IFCGRID('2$abcdefghijklmnopqrst1',$,$,$,$,#10,$,(#1),(#2),$,.NOTDEFINED.);",
        "#30=IFCVIRTUALGRIDINTERSECTION((#1,#2),(0.,0.,0.));",
        "#40=IFCGRIDPLACEMENT(#30,$);"
      ],
      "expect": [
        "#20=IFCGRID('2$abcdefghijklmnopqrst1',$,$,$,$,#10,$,(#1),(#2),$,.NOTDEFINED.);",
        "#30=IFCVIRTUALGRIDINTERSECTION((#1,#2),(0.,0.,0.));",
        "#40=IFCGRIDPLACEMENT(#10,#30,$);"
      ]
    },
